  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_chair_summary_v1.md",
  "summary": {
    "leading_positions": [
      {
        "ballot_ids": [
          "B009"
        ],
        "explicit_supporters": [
          "eriksson-olle",
          "johansson-goran",
          "kroll-stefan",
          "lindroth-eva",
          "pearce-mark"
        ],
        "synthesis": "These members prefer optical lattice clocks credited to Hidetoshi Katori and Jun Ye. They share the view that the magic-wavelength concept, decisive experimental development, long-term independent reproducibility, and durable metrological use make this a mature and bounded achievement. They also share the need to delimit foundational work from subsequent community advances and to test whether two names adequately capture indispensable early contributions."
      },
      {
        "ballot_ids": [
          "B043"
        ],
        "explicit_supporters": [
          "danielsson-ulf"
        ],
        "synthesis": "Danielsson prefers WMAP, credited to Charles L. Bennett, David N. Spergel, and Lyman A. Page Jr., for its precision cosmological measurements and subsequent testing. This position accepts its maturity but identifies a substantial attribution issue in representing a large mission, analysis, and theory collaboration with three laureates."
      },
      {
        "ballot_ids": [
          "B042"
        ],
        "explicit_supporters": [
          "mehlig-bernhard"
        ],
        "synthesis": "Mehlig prefers fluctuation theorems, credited to Christopher Jarzynski, Denis J. Evans, and Giovanni Gallavotti, as a mature, universal and experimentally relevant advance in nonequilibrium physics. The position requires a sharper historical division between the original relation, later generalizations, and the proposed laureates' distinct roles."
      },
      {
        "ballot_ids": [
          "B025"
        ],
        "explicit_supporters": [
          "olsson-eva"
        ],
        "synthesis": "Olsson prefers qPlus force microscopy, credited to Franz J. Giessibl, Gerhard Meyer, and Leo Gross, as a mature instrumental advance enabling atomic-scale chemical identification. Its principal unresolved issue is whether the proposed trio can be cleanly separated from the broader atomic-force-microscopy lineage and later chemical-imaging work."
      }
    ],
    "areas_of_agreement": [
      "B009 is regarded across the round-one statements as a strong, mature alternative or preferred configuration, particularly because of its independent reproducibility and durable precision-measurement role.",
      "For B009, any citation should remain confined to the foundational invention and development of optical lattice clocks rather than encompass later performance improvements, applications, or the entire optical-clock community.",
      "Historical attribution and a narrowly supportable citation are necessary before final consideration of the leading proposals."
    ],
    "scientific_disputes": [
      "Members differ over whether the strongest prize case is a bounded and reproducible precision-measurement platform (B009), a precision cosmological observational result (B043), a universal statistical-physics relation (B042), or a narrowly defined microscopy capability (B025).",
      "B001 is valued for its conceptual importance, but members differ over whether its broad relation to later atomic, ion, neutral-atom, and platform-specific programmes makes it less cleanly bounded than B009.",
      "B029 remains a serious alternative for several members, but its distinct scientific framing relative to earlier recognition of topology remains under discussion."
    ],
    "attribution_disputes": [
      "Whether Katori and Ye alone capture the indispensable conceptual and experimental foundations of B009 without absorbing contributions from other early optical-clock researchers.",
      "Whether Bennett, Spergel, and Page can fairly represent the distributed WMAP mission, analysis, and theory effort, and how a citation can distinguish WMAP from the broader precision-cosmology programme.",
      "Whether the proposed trios for B042 and B025 accurately separate original contributions from later formulations, implementations, demonstrations, and wider research lineages.",
      "For B029, the attribution of theoretical and experimental credit and its boundary with earlier topological work require further review."
    ],
    "maturity_disputes": [
      "B009 is widely described as mature because of repeated independent performance demonstrations, whereas some members regard the broader quantum-computation implications associated with B001 as still unfolding.",
      "Lindroth considers B001 itself a highly mature theoretical achievement, while other members emphasize the incompleteness or breadth of the associated quantum-computing programme.",
      "Danielsson treats later independent tests of WMAP's conclusions as evidence of maturity, while the remaining concern is chiefly attribution rather than the durability of its cosmological impact."
    ],
    "conflict_or_recusal_flags": [],
    "questions_for_round2": [
      "Can archival review establish whether Katori and Ye alone represent the indispensable invention and foundational experimental development of optical lattice clocks, and what exact citation boundary follows from that record?",
      "For WMAP, can documentary evidence support the proposed three-person attribution while distinguishing the mission's decisive measurements from the wider precision-cosmology programme?",
      "For fluctuation theorems and qPlus microscopy, what priority and contribution record supports the proposed three-person configurations rather than a narrower or differently constituted attribution?",
      "How should B001 and B029 be bounded against overlapping later platform-specific achievements and earlier topological recognition, respectively?"
    ],
    "chair_observation": "The round has a clear five-member preference for B009, but it has also identified several mature minority proposals whose principal outstanding questions concern the fair and historically precise allocation of credit."
  }
}
